==English==
{{was wotd|2016|June|20}}
 

===Etymology===
Believed to be from a quotation by the British-Irish statesman and philosopher {{w|Edmund Burke}} (1729–1797): “Falsehood and delusion are allowed in no case whatever: But, as in the exercise of all the virtues, there is an œconomy of truth. It is a sort of temperance, by which a man speaks truth with measure that he may speak it the longer.”<ref>{{cite-book|author=Edmund Burke|authorlink=Edmund Burke|chapter=Letter I. On the Overtures of Peace.|title=Two Letters Addressed to a Member of the Present Parliament, on the Proposals for Peace with the Regicide Directory of France|edition=10th|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Rivington (publishers)|F[rancis] and C[harles] Rivington]], [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-yard]]|year=1796|page=137|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0aFYAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA137|oclc=187301184}}.</ref>

